1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:13,376
This is STAT 331, video 5 b. In video 5 a, we found the maximum likelihood estimator of sigma squared: the sum of squared residuals, divided by n.

2
00:00:13,826 --> 00:00:20,269
This video prepares you for two question types. Each type gets a method card and worked problems.

3
00:00:20,719 --> 00:00:31,940
Type C: compute sigma squared hat and sigma hat, from residuals or from computer output. Tutorial 1 asks this in questions 1 c and 2 b.

4
00:00:32,390 --> 00:00:43,334
Type D: show that S squared, which divides by n minus 2, is an unbiased estimator of sigma squared. This is Tutorial 1, question 3.

5
00:00:43,784 --> 00:00:59,187
Video 5 a found sigma squared hat M L, the sum of e i squared over n. Slide 35 says that we usually use another estimator: the sum of e i squared over n minus 2. Why?

6
00:00:59,637 --> 00:01:19,199
Here is our own simulation. We take the five x values 1 to 5. We generate y from a true line with sigma squared equal to 1. We repeat this 20,000 times. Each time, we calculate sigma squared hat M L. The red histogram shows the 20,000 values.

7
00:01:19,649 --> 00:01:32,556
The purple line is the true value, 1. The average of the estimates is 0.60. So the maximum likelihood estimator is too small on average. We call it biased.

8
00:01:33,006 --> 00:01:47,299
Now divide by n minus 2, which is 3, in place of 5. The average of these estimates is 1.00. This estimator is unbiased: its expected value is sigma squared.

9
00:01:47,749 --> 00:02:11,771
Slide 36 gives the reason. It uses one fact: the sum of e i squared, over sigma squared, has a chi squared distribution with n minus 2 degrees of freedom. The proof comes later in the course. The mean of a chi squared distribution is its degrees of freedom. For n equals 5, the curve has 3 degrees of freedom, and its mean is 3.

10
00:02:12,221 --> 00:02:20,818
The blue histogram is the sum of squared residuals from our simulation, with sigma squared equal to 1. It follows the curve.

11
00:02:21,268 --> 00:02:27,028
Now the expectation of sigma squared hat. Multiply and divide by sigma squared.

12
00:02:27,478 --> 00:02:36,353
Take the constant 1 over n minus 2 out of the expectation. The framed part is the chi squared variable of line 1.

13
00:02:36,803 --> 00:02:40,301
Its expectation is n minus 2, by line 1.

14
00:02:40,751 --> 00:02:47,535
n minus 2 cancels. So the expectation is sigma squared: sigma squared hat is unbiased.

15
00:02:47,985 --> 00:03:04,412
The same steps with n give n minus 2 over n, times sigma squared, for the M L estimator. For n equals 5, this is 3 over 5, which is 0.6. This agrees with the simulation average, 0.60.

16
00:03:04,862 --> 00:03:15,785
This slide is optional. It is not on the slides. It gives a picture of the n minus 2. Take n equals 5. Suppose you know three of the residuals.

17
00:03:16,235 --> 00:03:29,013
The two constraints of video 5 a, the sum of e i equals 0 and the sum of x i e i equals 0, are two linear equations. They fix the last two residuals.

18
00:03:29,463 --> 00:03:41,773
So only 5 minus 2, which is 3, residuals are free. We estimated two parameters, beta zero and beta one. This is why the denominator is n minus 2.

19
00:03:42,223 --> 00:03:56,836
The two estimators differ only in the denominator. Their ratio is n over n minus 2. For n equals 5, the ratio is 5 over 3, which is 1.67. The difference is large.

20
00:03:57,286 --> 00:04:09,553
For n equals 50, the ratio is 50 over 48, which is 1.04. So slide 36 says: often it does not matter when n is 50 or more.

21
00:04:10,003 --> 00:04:20,819
The Gladstone data have n equals 236. The ratio is 236 over 234, which is 1.009.

22
00:04:21,269 --> 00:04:43,455
For the Gladstone data, the sum of squared residuals is 1,224,901. Divided by 234, the square root is 72.35 grams. Divided by 236, it is 72.04 grams. The two values are almost equal.

23
00:04:43,905 --> 00:04:54,295
Section 2. Question type C asks you to compute sigma squared hat and sigma hat. The input is a list of residuals, or computer output.

24
00:04:54,745 --> 00:05:04,771
Step 1 of method card C: S S E, the sum of the squared residuals. Square the residuals and add them, or read the sum from the output.

25
00:05:05,221 --> 00:05:12,773
Step 2: the degrees of freedom are n minus 2. We estimated two parameters, beta zero and beta one.

26
00:05:13,223 --> 00:05:18,194
Step 3: sigma squared hat is S S E divided by n minus 2.

27
00:05:18,644 --> 00:05:24,831
Step 4: sigma hat is the square root of sigma squared hat. It has the units of y.

28
00:05:25,281 --> 00:05:34,134
Step 5: check. In R output, the line residual standard error gives sigma hat, on n minus 2 degrees of freedom.

29
00:05:34,584 --> 00:05:46,893
A type C question. Pause the video and try it. Tutorial 1, question 1 c, needs sigma squared hat before the standard error. The residuals come from video 5 a.

30
00:05:47,343 --> 00:06:00,037
Step 1 of card C: square each residual. Use the unrounded values. In row 1, 2.6932 squared is 7.253.

31
00:06:00,487 --> 00:06:13,756
Add the squares: S S E is 104.1. In the picture, each square has the area of one squared residual. The month with 88 hours gives the largest square.

32
00:06:14,206 --> 00:06:18,195
Step 2: the degrees of freedom. 7 minus 2 is 5.

33
00:06:18,645 --> 00:06:25,067
Step 3: sigma squared hat is 104.1 over 5, which is 20.82.

34
00:06:25,517 --> 00:06:36,333
Step 4: sigma hat is the square root of 20.82, which is 4.563 hundred dollars, about 456 dollars.

35
00:06:36,783 --> 00:06:47,044
Step 5: check. The tutorial solution gives 20.82 too. Video 6 uses this value for the standard error of beta one hat.

36
00:06:47,494 --> 00:07:01,169
Another type C question, now from computer output. Pause the video and try it. Tutorial 1, question 2 b, gives R output for 100 aircraft. Verify the estimate of sigma.

37
00:07:01,619 --> 00:07:11,880
Step 1 of card C: the output gives S S E, the sum of the squared residuals: 17,818,085.

38
00:07:12,330 --> 00:07:19,797
Step 2: the degrees of freedom. 100 minus 2 is 98. The output shows the same 98.

39
00:07:20,247 --> 00:07:31,788
Step 3: sigma squared hat is 17,818,085 over 98, which is 181,817.2.

40
00:07:32,238 --> 00:07:38,382
Step 4: sigma hat is the square root: 426.40 thousand dollars.

41
00:07:38,832 --> 00:07:51,760
Step 5: check. The output says: residual standard error, 426. Our 426.40 rounds to 426. They agree.

42
00:07:52,210 --> 00:08:07,549
The slides compute sigma hat for the Gladstone data. Pause the video and try it. On the left are the 236 people and the fitted line. On the right is a histogram of the 236 residuals.

43
00:08:07,999 --> 00:08:20,351
Step 1 of card C: square the 236 residuals and add them. From the data file, S S E is 1,224,901.

44
00:08:20,801 --> 00:08:34,945
Steps 2 and 3: n minus 2 is 234. 1,224,901 over 234 is 5234.6.

45
00:08:35,395 --> 00:08:52,845
Step 4: sigma hat is the square root of 5234.6, which is 72.35 grams. Lecture 3 shows the same value. The green band goes from sigma hat below the line to sigma hat above it.

46
00:08:53,295 --> 00:09:10,703
The dashed lines on the histogram are at minus and plus sigma hat. Sigma hat measures the typical size of a residual. Here, 166 of the 236 residuals, about 70 percent, are inside these lines.

47
00:09:11,153 --> 00:09:28,775
Section 3. Question type D asks you to prove that S squared, the sum of squared residuals over n minus 2, is unbiased. This is Tutorial 1, question 3. We do not use the chi squared fact. We use only means and variances.

48
00:09:29,225 --> 00:09:41,662
Step 1 of method card D: the goal. Show that the expected sum of squared residuals is n minus 2 times sigma squared. Then the expected S squared is sigma squared.

49
00:09:42,112 --> 00:09:49,621
Step 2: write the sum of squared residuals as S y y minus beta one hat squared S x x.

50
00:09:50,071 --> 00:09:54,679
Step 3: find the expected value of S y y, from the model.

51
00:09:55,129 --> 00:10:08,633
Step 4: find the expected value of beta one hat squared, times S x x. Use: the expected W squared is the variance of W plus the squared mean of W.

52
00:10:09,083 --> 00:10:14,395
Step 5: subtract the two expectations, and divide by n minus 2.

53
00:10:14,845 --> 00:10:31,848
Now Tutorial 1, question 3, with card D. Pause the video and try it. Step 1: the goal. Show that the expected sum of squared residuals is n minus 2 times sigma squared. Then S squared has expectation sigma squared.

54
00:10:32,298 --> 00:10:45,333
Step 2: write the sum of squared residuals in a new form. Start with the fitted value: beta zero hat plus beta one hat x i. The framed beta zero hat changes next.

55
00:10:45,783 --> 00:11:05,751
Put in beta zero hat equals y bar minus beta one hat x bar. So y i hat is y bar plus beta one hat times x i minus x bar. In the picture, the line goes through the mean point, and the green part is beta one hat times x i minus x bar.

56
00:11:06,201 --> 00:11:21,113
Subtract from y i. The residual is y i minus y bar, minus beta one hat times x i minus x bar. In the picture, the orange residual is the blue distance minus the green part.

57
00:11:21,563 --> 00:11:39,824
Square and add over i. The square of a difference gives three sums: S y y, minus 2 beta one hat S x y, plus beta one hat squared S x x. The bar shows S y y for the Tutorial 1 data: 322.

58
00:11:40,274 --> 00:11:47,805
Beta one hat is S x y over S x x. So S x y equals beta one hat S x x.

59
00:11:48,255 --> 00:12:08,308
Collect the two terms. The sum of squared residuals is S y y minus beta one hat squared S x x. Check with the Tutorial 1 data: 322 minus 217.9 is 104.1, the sum of squared residuals from before.

60
00:12:08,758 --> 00:12:20,342
Step 3 of card D: the expected value of S y y. Write y i with the model, and also its mean, y bar. Subtract. Beta zero cancels.

61
00:12:20,792 --> 00:12:26,317
Square and add over i. S y y has three terms. The picture lists them.

62
00:12:26,767 --> 00:12:35,279
The first term, beta one squared S x x, is a constant, because the x i are fixed. Its expectation is itself.

63
00:12:35,729 --> 00:12:46,311
The middle term is a sum of fixed numbers times epsilon i minus epsilon bar. Each epsilon has mean 0. So its expectation is 0.

64
00:12:46,761 --> 00:12:56,830
Add the parts. The last term, the sum of epsilon i minus epsilon bar, squared, is left. The next slide finds its expectation.

65
00:12:57,280 --> 00:13:08,245
Now the expectation of the sum of epsilon i minus epsilon bar, squared. Expand the square. The framed sum of epsilon i changes next.

66
00:13:08,695 --> 00:13:21,303
The sum of epsilon i is n epsilon bar. So the middle term is minus 2 n epsilon bar squared. With the last term, this leaves minus n epsilon bar squared.

67
00:13:21,753 --> 00:13:37,156
Each epsilon i has mean 0 and variance sigma squared. So the expected epsilon i squared is sigma squared. The n terms give n sigma squared: the n orange blocks, drawn for n equals 5.

68
00:13:37,606 --> 00:13:53,713
Epsilon bar also has mean 0. The epsilons are independent, so the variance of epsilon bar is sigma squared over n. So n epsilon bar squared has expectation sigma squared. This removes one block.

69
00:13:54,163 --> 00:13:59,496
So the expectation is n minus 1 times sigma squared: the green part.

70
00:13:59,946 --> 00:14:09,247
Put this into part 1. The expected S y y is beta one squared S x x, plus n minus 1 times sigma squared.

71
00:14:09,697 --> 00:14:29,943
Step 4 of card D: the expected value of beta one hat squared, times S x x. First write beta one hat as a linear function of the y i. The sum of x i minus x bar, times y bar, is 0, so y bar drops out. Slide 26 has this step.

72
00:14:30,393 --> 00:14:36,643
Take the expectation. The expected y i is beta zero plus beta one x i.

73
00:14:37,093 --> 00:14:53,627
The sum of x i minus x bar, times beta zero, is 0. The sum of x i minus x bar, times x i, is S x x. So the expected beta one hat is beta one: the centre of its distribution.

74
00:14:54,077 --> 00:15:09,629
The y i are independent, each with variance sigma squared. The variance of a sum of constants times y i is the sum of the squared constants times sigma squared. This gives sigma squared over S x x.

75
00:15:10,079 --> 00:15:25,439
For any random variable W, the expected W squared is its variance plus its squared mean. So the expected beta one hat squared, times S x x, is sigma squared plus beta one squared S x x.

76
00:15:25,889 --> 00:15:44,107
Step 5 of card D: subtract. Take the expectation of step 2. The expected sum of squared residuals is the expected S y y minus the expected beta one hat squared S x x. The top bar is the expected S y y.

77
00:15:44,557 --> 00:15:53,923
Put in the results of steps 3 and 4. The second bar is what we subtract: beta one squared S x x, plus one sigma squared.

78
00:15:54,373 --> 00:16:03,717
Beta one squared S x x cancels. One block of sigma squared also cancels. n minus 2 blocks of sigma squared are left.

79
00:16:04,167 --> 00:16:09,095
Divide by n minus 2. The expected S squared is sigma squared.

80
00:16:09,545 --> 00:16:19,998
So S squared is an unbiased estimator of sigma squared. The proof uses only the means, the variances, and the independence of the errors.

81
00:16:20,448 --> 00:16:33,184
To recap. Card C computes sigma hat: S S E, n minus 2 degrees of freedom, sigma squared hat, its square root, and the check with the residual standard error in the output.

82
00:16:33,634 --> 00:16:48,290
Card D proves that S squared is unbiased: the goal, the new form of the sum of squared residuals, the expected S y y, the expected beta one hat squared S x x, and the subtraction.

83
00:16:48,740 --> 00:17:08,004
The maximum likelihood estimator divides by n and is biased. The estimator that divides by n minus 2 is unbiased. For 50 or more observations, the two are almost equal. The next video covers the mean, variance, and distribution of beta one hat.
